UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 3, Lesson 3: Place value problem solving', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Place-value problem solving uses digit values and regrouping to answer real questions about quantity, not digit tricks alone.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nThere are 3,060 stickers in packs of 10. How many packs? Show place reasoning.\n\nFEEDBACK\n306 packs — 3,060 ones = 306 tens.\n\nWATCH FOR\nDividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSolve four place-value word problems with new numbers (boxes of 10 and 100, leftover ones, comparing collections). Show working and a one-sentence reason for each.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Place-value problem solving uses digit values and regrouping to answer real questions about quantity, not digit tricks alone.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Problem solving","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-RN-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Solve four place-value word problems with new numbers (boxes of 10 and 100, leftover ones, comparing collections). Show working and a one-sentence reason for each. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for problem; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts.. Correction uses 306 packs — 3,060 ones = 306 tens.. Why: restores accurate problem.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses problem with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with problem, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for problem, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about problem, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for problem. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["306 packs — 3,060 ones = 306 tens","Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"There are 3,060 stickers in packs of 10. How many packs? Show place reasoning. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for problem; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts","Place-value problem solving uses digit values and regrouping to answer real questions about quantity, not digit tricks","306 packs — 3,060 ones = 306 tens","Careful use of place value and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named place value and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply problem to new material and name the place value and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts","Name place value and explain why it matters only, without linking it to problem"],"question":"Which response best applies problem with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Write only a final number with no working","Change digits to look neater without checking place value","Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts","Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught problem method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore units and treat 3,060 and 10 as interchangeable","306 packs — 3,060 ones = 306 tens","Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts","Read 3,060 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value"],"question":"Using the method for problem, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Disagreement is resolved by checking places, operations and the question focus, not by blending answers. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Delete units so the answers appear to match","Average the two answers without checking either","Recheck places, operations and whether the question asked for the quantity you computed","Pick the longer-looking working automatically"],"question":"If two methods disagree on a problem problem with numbers such as 3,060 and 10, what should you check first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Transfer requires new numbers with the same mathematical method and a named reason. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked model numbers 3,060 and 10 as the answer","Change only handwriting and keep the same numbers","Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts","Use the same problem method on new numbers and name the places or values that matter"],"question":"Solve a fresh problem problem that uses different numbers from the worked model and explain one key step.?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Recognising the warned misconception protects accuracy on new problems. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["306 packs — 3,060 ones = 306 tens","Label units when comparing 3,060 with 10","Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts","Place-value problem solving uses digit values and regrouping to answer real questions about quantity, not digit tricks"],"question":"Which error is the lesson warning against for problem?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for problem, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers.","question":"Solve one fresh problem that uses problem with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for problem; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about problem, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with problem, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts.. Correction uses 306 packs — 3,060 ones = 306 tens.. Why: restores accurate problem.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w03-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise problem.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use problem with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify place value and explain why it matters.","I can practise reason independently on a new prompt.","I can notice model needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Place-value problem solving uses digit values and regrouping to answer real questions about quantity, not digit tricks alone.","options":["A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working","Place-value problem solving uses digit values and regrouping to answer real questions about quantity, not digit tricks"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about problem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA school has 2,450 pencils. A box holds 100. How many full boxes? 24 full boxes with 50 left over, because 24 hundreds make 2,400.\n\nTRY FIRST\nThere are 3,060 stickers in packs of 10. How many packs? Show place reasoning.\n\nFEEDBACK\n306 packs — 3,060 ones = 306 tens.","explain":"Place-value problem solving uses digit values and regrouping to answer real questions about quantity, not digit tricks alone.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Place-value division into hundreds boxes.","caption":"Use hundreds and leftovers, not digit tricks.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">2,450 pencils</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">boxes of 100</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Full boxes</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">24 with 50 left</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: A school has 2,450 pencils. A box holds 100. How many full boxes? 24 full boxes with 50 left over, because 24 hundreds m Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: A school has 2,450 pencils. A box holds 100. How many full boxes? 24 full boxes with 50 left over, because 24 hundreds m Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about problem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA school has 2,450 pencils. A box holds 100. How many full boxes? 24 full boxes with 50 left over, because 24 hundreds make 2,400. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Ones regrouped as tens for pack counts.","caption":"Place meaning drives the operation.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">3,060 stickers</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">packs of 10</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Packs</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">306</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about problem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts. Here is a more productive model: A school has 2,450 pencils. A box holds 100. How many full boxes? 24 full boxes with 50 left over, because 24 hundreds make 2,400.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\n306 packs — 3,060 ones = 306 tens.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Dividing digits separately (3, 0, 6) without place meaning gives nonsense pack counts. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about problem with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPlace-value problem solving uses digit values and regrouping to answer real questions about 